A finite Boolean atom, built from an enumerated family of neighbourhoods of a positive difference-closed neighbourhood system and taken relative to the master, is empty or a neighbourhood.

At depth zero the atom is the master. At a successor, the next branch is either the next enumerated neighbourhood or the difference of the master with that neighbourhood, hence empty or a neighbourhood by difference closure. The intersection of the inductive atom with that branch is empty if either factor is empty. If both factors are neighbourhoods, the intersection is either empty or, when nonempty, a neighbourhood by positivity.

The first output of a splitting operation satisfying the abstract splitting specification is contained in the set being split.

That specification equates the union of the two outputs with the set being split, and the first output is contained in the union.

The second output of a splitting operation satisfying the abstract splitting specification is contained in the set being split.

That specification equates the union of the two outputs with the set being split, and the second output is contained in the union.

The recovered set YPseq i is contained in YPseq j if and only if the enumerated set Y i is contained in Y j.

Specialize the combined inclusion transfer to the odd indices 2i + 1 and 2j + 1. At those indices the interleaved families are YPseq and Y. Intersecting with the masters does not change them, because YPseq i is contained in the master of the first system and Y i is contained in the master of the second.

If the two neighbourhood systems are positive, difference-closed, and atomless, their masters are nonempty, and the enumerations cover the neighbourhoods and begin with the masters, then the two systems are isomorphic.

The domain isomorphism assembled for this exercise is a witness of that isomorphism.
